      "text": "To determine the length of the tunnel, we need to first calculate the total distance the train travels while the tail is exiting the tunnel after the front has entered. Given that the train is moving at a speed of 60 miles per hour, we can use this information to find the distance covered.\n\nStarting with the given data:\n- Length of the train = 1 mile\n- Distance covered by the train (from the front entering to the tail exiting the tunnel) = 1 mile + 1 mile = 2 miles\n\nThe total distance covered by the train is 2 miles. Since the train covers this distance in 3 minutes, we convert this time to hours to find the rate at which the train is traveling. We know that there are 60 minutes in an hour:\n\n\\[ 3 \\text{ minutes} = \\frac{3}{60} \\text{ hours} \\]\n\nNext, we convert the travel rate from miles per hour to miles per minute:\n\n\\[ 60 \\text{ miles per hour} = \\frac{60 \\text{ miles}}{60 \\text{ minutes}} = 1 \\text{ mile per minute} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the total distance covered (which is the sum of the distance the train travels on both sides of the tunnel):\n\n\\[ \\text{Total distance covered} = 2 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nNext, we calculate the time taken to exit the tunnel:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time to exit the tunnel} = 3 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nAnd similarly, we need to find the time taken to enter the tunnel, which is the same as the time taken to exit, so:\n\n\\[ \\text{Time to enter the tunnel} = 3 \\text{ minutes} \\]\n\nTo find the length of the tunnel, we need to consider the distance the train actually travels within the tunnel. This distance is the head-to-tail distance of the train, which is twice its length:\n\n\\[ \\text{Length of the tunnel} = 2 \\times 2 = 4 \\text{ miles} \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the tunnel is 4 miles. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]",
